00:00:03.619 --> 00:00:06.950 align:start position:0%
a ubiquitous chemical called bisphenol A  better known as BPA coats the inside of  many food and drink cans this lining is  two micrometers thick less than 1/8 of  the thickness of a human hair it's the  crucial barrier between metal and liquid  that ensures a can of soda will retain  its fizz and flavor because it's cheap  durable and flexible BPA is a key  building block of the plastics we use  but there are significant health  concerns related to BPA a lab at Sherwin  Williams a company better known for  paint has come up with an alternative  BPA has been linked to breast and  prostate cancer and reduce fertility  studies have shown that it mimics the  hormone estrogen  which influences growth and development  and disrupts other endocrine processes  meanwhile in the 2000s the CDC found BPA  in the urine of 93 percent of u.s.  adults tested a public backlash to the  use of BPA has ensued today certain BPA  products are banned in many countries  companies have sought a good replacement  for BPA as a can liner but have  struggled vinyls and acrylics have  shorter shelf lives or require more  coatings some other bisphenol come with  the same concerns as BPA but researchers  at Sherwin Williams came up with an idea  what if they could stick with a  bisphenol but find one that wasn't an  endocrine disruptor like BPA company  scientists started out with a computer  program it screened hundreds of  different bisphenol x'  searching for molecular structures that  appeared unlikely to bind with the  body's estrogen receptors the team found  a promising molecule tetramethyl  bisphenol f TM BPF a rarely used  chemical once considered for helping  build electronic circuit boards research  suggests that one of the advantages of  TM BPF is that it doesn't leak from can  linings into drinks like BPA does next  Sherwin Williams wanted proof that the  chemical was benign the company wanted  to establish the molecule safety and  protect its research investment so the  developers decided that they should let  outside scientists and consumer  advocates in on the process the lab  reached out to Anna so tow and  endocrinologists well-known for raising  concerns about ppas health effects she  put the chemical through a battery of  tests her lab found that TM BPF didn't  activate estrogen receptors or spur  growth of breast cancer cells and didn't  affect several genes that respond to  estrogen sure when Williams has taken a  hands-off approach so that Soto could  study the chemical however she saw fit  this has added legitimacy to the  research in the eyes of the broader  community another scientist reviewed  test data and organ samples and  concluded that TM BPF doesn't seem to  affect the thyroid system but it wasn't  all good news one scientists found that  TM BPF might blunt the effect of  estrogen on test cells and that a  polymer made from the molecule might  have similar effects on testosterone  however a lead researcher in the study  said the findings weren't conclusive and  could be specific to these cells  environmentalists are cautiously  encouraged about TM BPF but they aren't  assured that it is safer than other  options something as complex as the  endocrine system has made a definitive  safety screening out of  reach so far still other scientists are  working on more tests and  sherwin-williams is preparing to launch  a new study to answer some of the  remaining questions meanwhile the  company has already started selling a TM  BPF based can coating branded as v70 the  company says that since 2017 its product  has been used on 22 billion cans still a  modest fraction of the estimated 350  billion cans produced each year  worldwide regardless of public health  concerns the bottom line might be what  determines the adoption of these 70 it's  currently twice the price of bpa though  company officials say this is comparable  to other non bpa linings it remains to  be seen whether or not both industry and  consumer groups will ultimately deem the  chemical to be safer either way the  price tag could keep this BPA  alternative from becoming as popular as  BPA  you  [Music]
 

